\title[Inadmissibility of sign-blind variance-share reporting]
{Inadmissibility of Sign-Blind Variance-Share Reporting\\
\normalsize The exact invariant content, ambiguity amplitude, and one-bit repair
of the augmentation increment}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{12 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a finite weighted population with a base predictor $x$ (the \emph{footprint}), a
candidate predictor $z$ (the \emph{feature}), and a target $y$ (the \emph{rate}), the
\emph{augmentation increment} $\Delta R^2$ measures the additional share of the target's
variance explained when $z$ is adjoined to a model already containing $x$. Reporting
protocols overwhelmingly publish variance shares $R^2(x,y)$, $R^2(z,y)$, $R^2(x,z)$,
which are squares of the corresponding correlations and hence \emph{sign-blind}. We
determine exactly how much of the increment survives this loss. We prove that no
function of the three variance shares returns $\Delta R^2$ on all finite populations,
already failing on four-key populations; that the witnessing configurations contain an
open set of moment space; that the ambiguity is quantised, two populations with
identical readings either reporting the same increment or increments differing by
exactly $4\sqrt{R^2(x,y)R^2(z,y)R^2(x,z)}/(1-R^2(x,z))$; that this amplitude vanishes
precisely when one reading is zero; that within an explicit two-parameter family the
concealable share has greatest value $16/17$, attained at $b=1$, $u=\sqrt5$; that
universally the amplitude is at most $2\sqrt{R^2(x,z)}/(1+\sqrt{R^2(x,z)})<1$, a ceiling
derived from Cauchy--Schwarz through the nonnegativity of the correlation Gram
determinant; and that the defect is exactly one bit deep, being repaired by logging the
sign of the correlation triple product $P=\rho_{xy}\rho_{xz}\rho_{zy}$.
\end{abstract}

